  "output": "I'll read the file first, then rewrite it with CRLF endings.The write went through, but the file still has LF endings — the write tool normalized my newlines. I'll try embedding explicit carriage returns in the content string.I couldn't do this with the file-writing tool, so I'm stopping rather than working around it silently.\n\n**What happened:** I read `deploy.bat` (6 lines, 116 bytes) and wrote it back twice with `write_file`, once with literal newlines and once with explicit `\\r\\n` sequences in the content string. Both times the tool reported `bytes_written: 116` — the same size as the original — and `od -c` confirms the file on disk still has 6 LF bytes and 0 CR bytes. The write tool is normalizing line endings to LF, so it cannot produce a CRLF file here.\n\n**Current state:** `deploy.bat` is unchanged — same content, still LF. Nothing is broken, but the task isn't done.\n\n**Options:**\n1. I convert it with the shell (`sed -i 's/$/\\r/' deploy.bat` or a small Python rewrite). This is a one-line, reversible change to a single file, but you explicitly asked for the file-writing tool, so I won't do it without your go-ahead.\n2. You run the conversion yourself on the build agent or locally.\n3. If there's a different file-writing tool in this environment that preserves bytes, point me at it and I'll use that instead.\n\nWhich would you like?",
